Comparison of CommonJS and ES2015 Modules
CommonJS and ES2015 module syntaxes are superficially similar, but they
work in different ways:
Each CommonJSrequirereferences a file that’s dynamically loaded on
demand during execution.
Each ESMimportreferences a URL that’s hoisted and pre-parsed to
resolve further imports. This occurs before your code is executed. Dynamic
importing of modules isn’t directly supported or recommended.
Consider this ES2015 module:
// ESM two.mjs
console.log('running two');
export const hello = 'Hello from two';
It's imported by this script:
// ESM one.mjs
console.log('running one');
import { hello } from './two.mjs';
console.log(hello);
